  "query_preview": "..A trough and its associated area of low pressure\nwill continue to depart the forecast area through the next 12\nhours, bringing increasingly stable conditions across Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming. With high pressure building in from the north\nand low pressure departing to the south, an increased surface\npressure gradient across southern Utah will help to drive stronger\nwinds through terrain features (particularly in southwestern\nUtah). These breezy winds, specifically around the Pine Valley\nMountains, will continue through the remainder of the evening and\novernight hours, gradually tapering off through Saturday morning\nas the aforementioned area of low pressure continues its track\nsouth.\n\nHigh pressure will spread across the area throughout the day on\nSaturday, bringing dry and stable conditions areawide. With\nwarming temperatures overhead, anticipate a gradual warming trend\nthrough the weekend alongside largely clear skies. As we're\ngetting further into the cool season, warming temperatures\noverhead begin to raise concern for persistent valley inversions.\nAs such, would anticipate at least a little bit of valley haze\nalong the Wasatch Front by the end of the weekend or the beginning\nof next week as this area of high pressure looks to remain in\nplace through at least Monday. \n\nModels indicate potential for a chunk of northern stream energy to\ngraze by northern Utah/ southern ID on Tuesday, introducing a\ncooler airmass that will help to bring potential for valley\ninversions to break or, at the very least, weaken. This is going\nto be the most noteworthy impact of this weak shortwave as\nmoisture is severely lacking. If the trough helps to break the \ninversion, then whatever urban haze has accumulated will have a \nchance to dissipate. However, if this wave comes in weaker and/ or\ntracks farther north, then we'll likely continue to see the\naccumulation of valley pollutants from now through the end of next\nweek as high pressure looks to quickly reestablish over the region\nfrom mid-week forward. \n\nModels do indicate strong potential for the pattern to shift/\nbreak down as we head into late next week/ next weekend.",
